Anton Kuznietsov received the B.Sc. and M.Sc. degrees in computational engineering from the Technical University of Darmstadt, Darmstadt, Germany, in 2020 and 2022, respectively. Since 2023, he has been a Research Associate with the Institute of Automotive Engineering, Technical University of Darmstadt. His research interests include explainable AI and AI safety in autonomous driving.
Balint Gyevnar received the Master of Informatics degree (Hons.) from the University of Edinburgh in 2021, where he is currently pursuing the Ph.D. degree with the Autonomous Agents Research Group, under the supervision of Stefano Albrecht, Shay Cohen, and Christopher Lucas. His research interests include human-centred explainable methods for multi-agent systems, focusing on causality, conversational agents, and AI safety for human-aligned trust calibration.
Cheng Wang (Member, IEEE) received the B.Sc. degree from the School of Automotive Engineering, Wuhan University of Technology, Wuhan, China, in 2014, the M.Sc. degree from the School of Automotive Studies, Tongji University, Shanghai, China, in 2017, and the Ph.D. degree from the Institute of Automotive Engineering, Technical University of Darmstadt, Darmstadt, Germany, in 2021. From 2022 to 2024, he was a Research Associate with the University of Edinburgh. He is currently an Assistant Professor with Heriot-Watt University, Edinburgh. His research interests include explainable AI and safety verification and validation of autonomous vehicles.
Steven Peters was born in 1987. He received the Ph.D. (Dr.-Ing.) degree from Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, Karlsruhe, Baden-Württemberg, Germany, in 2013. From 2016 to 2022, he was the Manager of Artificial Intelligence Research with Mercedes-Benz AG, Germany. He has been a Full Professor with the Technical University of Darmstadt, Darmstadt, Germany, and heads the Institute of Automotive Engineering, Department of Mechanical Engineering, since 2022.
Stefano V. Albrecht received the B.Sc. degree in computer science from the Technical University of Darmstadt and the M.Sc. and Ph.D. degrees in AI from the University of Edinburgh. He is currently an Associate Professor in artificial intelligence with the School of Informatics, University of Edinburgh. He leads the Autonomous Agents Research Group (https://agents.inf.ed.ac.uk/) which develops machine learning algorithms for autonomous systems, including applications in autonomous driving. Previously, he was a Post-Doctoral Researcher with The University of Texas at Austin.

